For \(n\ge2\), put \(q=1-\frac1n\in(0,1)\). Using the given identity,
\[
0\le \log n-\sum_{k=1}^n\frac{q^k}{k}
=\sum_{k=n+1}^{\infty}\frac{q^k}{k}
\le \frac1n\sum_{k=n+1}^{\infty}q^k
=\frac{q^{n+1}}{n(1-q)}
=q^{n+1}\le1.
\]
Since \(\log n>0\), division gives
\[
1-\frac1{\log n}
\le \frac1{\log n}\sum_{k=1}^n\frac{(1-1/n)^k}{k}
\le1.
\]
Both bounds tend to \(1\), so the squeeze theorem applies.

Final answer: \(\boxed{1}\).

